<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-heat" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Heat and Temperature</title>
  <introduction>
   
  <p> 
    Heat is a form of energy that is transferred between systems due to a temperature difference. 
    Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance. 
    Temperature is an intensive property, while heat is an extensive property. 
    Heat can be transferred through three main mechanisms: conduction, convection, and radiation. 
    Conduction is the transfer of heat through direct contact between particles, where energy is 
    transferred from the hotter region to the cooler region. 
    Convection is the transfer of heat through the movement of a fluid, where the warmer fluid 
    rises and the cooler fluid sinks, creating a circulation pattern. Radiation is the transfer 
    of heat through electromagnetic waves, where energy is emitted by a hot object and absorbed 
    by a cooler object without the need for a medium. 
    The amount of heat transferred can be calculated using the equation <m>Q = m \times c \times \Delta T</m>, 
    where <m>Q</m> is the heat transferred, <m>m</m> is the mass of the substance, <m>c</m> is the specific heat capacity, 
    and <m>\Delta T</m> is the change in temperature.    
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>Specific heat capacity,</alert> <m>c</m> is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of one unit mass of a substance 
    by one degree Celsius. It is an intensive property that varies with the type of substance and its phase. 
    The specific heat capacity of a substance can be used to calculate the amount of heat required to change the temperature 
    of a given mass of the substance, as well as to determine the amount of heat released or absorbed during a phase change. 
    It is a heat absorbing property of a substance, and it is important in many applications, such as in the design of heating and cooling systems, 
    as well as in the study of thermodynamics and energy transfer. The amount of heat 
    absorbed by the system can be calculated using the equation 
    <m>Q = m \times c \times \Delta T</m>, 
    where <m>Q</m> is the heat absorbed, <m>m</m> is the mass of the substance, 
    <m>c</m> is the specific heat capacity, and <m>\Delta T</m> is the change in temperature. 
     In differential form, the equation can be expressed as 
    <me>
      \,dQ = mc\,dT
    </me>
    If we take <m>mc=C</m> then it is called a heat capacity of the substance, and the equation can be written as
    <me>
      \,dQ = C\,dT 
    </me> 
    or, 
    <men xml:id="heat_cap_eq1">
      C= \frac{ \,dQ}{\,dT}
    </men>
    Heat capacity at constant volume and pressure are denoted as <m>C_V</m> and <m>C_P</m> respectively, 
    and heat capacities are generally function of temperature. Heat capacity for a mole of substance 
    is called molar heat capacity, and it is denoted as <m>C_m</m>. The <m>C_V</m> and <m>C_P</m> 
    are related to each other by the equation <m>C_P - C_V = R</m>, where <m>R</m> is the ideal gas constant. 
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert> Latent heat,</alert> <m>L</m> is the amount of heat required to change the phase of a substance without changing its temperature. 
  It is an important concept in thermodynamics, as it explains the energy required for phase transitions such as melting, boiling, and condensation. 
  The latent heat of fusion is the amount of heat required to change a substance from a solid to a liquid, 
  while the latent heat of vaporization is the amount of heat required to change a substance from a liquid to a gas. 
  The latent heat of a substance can be calculated using the equation <m>Q = m \times L</m>, 
  where <m>Q</m> is the heat transferred, <m>m</m> is the mass of the substance, and <m>L</m> is the latent heat of the substance. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Heat is different from work as it is a form of energy transfer that occurs due to a temperature difference, 
    while work is a form of energy transfer that occurs due to a force acting over a distance. 
    Both Heat and work are path functions, meaning that the amount of heat transferred depends on the specific 
    path taken during the process. 
    The first law of thermodynamics states that the change in internal energy of a system is equal 
    to the heat added to the system minus the work done by the system  <m>\,dQ = \,dU + \,dW</m>. 
    This means that heat and work are both forms of energy transfer that can change the internal 
    energy of a system, and they are related to each other through the first law of thermodynamics. 
  </p>
 
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-laws_thermdynamics">
    <title>Laws of Thermodynamics</title>
    <p>
      The laws of thermodynamics are fundamental principles that govern the behavior of energy and matter in the universe. 
      The <term>first law</term> of thermodynamics, also known as the law of energy conservation, states that energy cannot be created 
      or destroyed, only transferred or converted from one form to another. This means that the total energy of 
      an isolated system remains constant over time. The <term>second law</term> of thermodynamics states that the total entropy 
      of an isolated system can never decrease over time, and it can only increase or remain constant. This means 
      that natural processes tend to move towards a state of greater disorder or randomness. 
      The <term>third law</term> of thermodynamics states that as the temperature of a system approaches absolute zero, 
      the entropy of the system approaches a minimum value. This means that it is impossible to reach absolute 
      zero in a finite number of steps.  <term>zeroth law</term> of thermodynamics states that if two systems are in thermal 
      equilibrium with a third system, then they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. 
      This means that if two systems have the same temperature, they are in thermal equilibrium with each other. 
      That is temperatures of all the substances in contact with each other will evantually reach a state of equilibrium.
    </p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-gas_laws">
    <title>The Gas Laws</title>
    <p>
      The gas laws are a set of mathematical relationships that describe the behavior of gases under different conditions. 
      The <term>Boyle's law</term> states that the pressure of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume at constant 
      temperature. This means that if the volume of a gas decreases, the pressure increases, and vice versa. 
      The <term>Charles's law</term> states that the volume of a gas is directly proportional to its 
      temperature at constant pressure. This means that if the temperature of a gas increases, the volume 
      increases, and vice versa. The <term>Gay-Lussac's law</term> states that the pressure of a gas is 
      directly proportional to its temperature at constant volume. This means that if the temperature of a 
      gas increases, the pressure increases, and vice versa. The <term>ideal gas law</term> combines 
      these three laws into a single equation, <m>PV = NRT</m>, where <m>P</m> is the pressure of the gas, 
      <m>V</m> is the volume of the gas, <m>N</m> is the number of moles of the gas, <m>R</m> is the ideal gas 
      constant, and <m>T</m> is the temperature of the gas. The ideal gas law is a useful tool for predicting 
      the behavior of gases under a wide range of conditions, although it is important to note that it is an 
      approximation and may not accurately describe the behavior of real gases under certain conditions, 
      such as high pressure or low temperature. 
    </p>
    
  </subsection>
</section>